<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Phra Si Sanphet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Phra Si Sanphet was the royal temple within the palace grounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, used for royal ceremonies and without resident monks. Founded in the fifteenth century, it is known for three great bell-shaped chedis that held the ashes of kings. It was destroyed by the Burmese in 1767 and is now part of the Historic City of Ayutthaya World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-phra-si-sanphet.jpg" alt="Wat Phra Si Sanphet" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Don Ramey Logan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Phra_Si_Sanphet_Ayutthaya_Historic_Park_Thailand_by_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
